    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4319e709_0a680fc4",
        "filename": "api/system-current.txt",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 3170,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-03-19T12:47:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is an important distinction to be made here between add* and set*. Typically it makes no sense to add null, but it makes sense to set to null. This is modulated by the specific type a method takes and what have the semantics of the specific call been so far.\n\nTo give an example, addRoute(null) should either be considered a programming error that crashes or a no-op for leniency, but in no case does it make sense to call addRoute(null). However something like setNat64Prefix(null) could make perfect sense in case a LP loses its prefix, assuming null codes for no prefix as opposed to e.g. a zero-length prefix coding for no prefix.\n\nGenerally speaking from an API design perspective I tend to think it\u0027s cleaner to have the set methods pass a non-null object that codes for nothing. An obvious example is setValidatedPrivateDnsServers, where an empty list will play that role in a less ambiguous sense than a null pointer (and let us have the list final as an added bonus).\nBut in this case LinkProperties has a lot of existing behavior and for stuff that has always historically treated null as the empty value, and I think we should keep the existing behavior in this case.\n\nAnyway, I went through all these APIs again and put my comments on the specific implementations in LinkProperties.",
      "parentUuid": "87885c6a_890b5adf",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3170,
        "startChar": 39,
        "endLine": 3170,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "bb132dbcd7aaf30a4872b3ae9ee3255dd46d0832",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
